GNU sed for OpenVMS
===================

This is GNU sed @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified GNU release plus the patches in the vms-sed project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-sed

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  GNU sed is free software under the GNU General
Public License, version 3 or later; see COPYING.  The full manual is in
SED.TXT (plain text) and sed.1 (man page source).

Setting up
----------
The kit installs SED$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define SED$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:SED$STARTUP.COM

Users then define the sed command in LOGIN.COM:

    $ @SED$ROOT:[000000]SED$SETUP.COM

Things that differ from Unix
----------------------------
- Quote the script.  With the default PARSE_STYLE=TRADITIONAL, DCL
  upper-cases an unquoted command line and the C run-time library then
  lower-cases it.  Put scripts and upper-case options in quotes:
      $ sed "s/Foo/Bar/g" file.txt
      $ sed "-E" "s/(a|b)+/x/" file.txt
  or use SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED.

- In-place editing.  sed -i "s/a/b/" file.txt writes a new version of the
  file and leaves the original as the previous version (PURGE removes it).
  sed -i.bak renames the original to file.txt.bak (file^.txt.bak on an ODS-5
  disk) and writes the edited file under the original name.  The rewritten
  file is Stream_LF, whatever the record format of the original.

- The e command (and s///e) runs its command through the C library's
  system(), which on OpenVMS is DCL: sed "1e SHOW TIME" runs a DCL command.

- Exit status.  sed exits with 0 (success), 1 (invalid command or option),
  2 (an input file could not be opened), 4 (I/O error), or the code given
  to q or Q.  DCL sees these as C-facility condition values; the code is
      $ code = ($STATUS .AND. %X7F8) / 8

- File names are reported in Unix form (dir/file.txt).

- Locales.  Choose one with a logical name or environment variable, e.g.
      $ DEFINE LC_ALL "UTF8-20"
  VMS has no "en_US.UTF-8"; use the generic UTF8-xx locales (see
  SYS$I18N_LOCALE:).  IA64 ships only UTF8-20, which covers characters of
  up to three bytes.

- sed sets several C run-time features at start-up (Unix file names, case
  preservation, ...).  Defining the corresponding DECC$ logical name
  yourself overrides sed's choice.
